  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "As of April 2026, Alabama Governor Kay Ivey signed SB57 into law, which moves to restrict the purchase of candy, soda, and certain high-caffeine energy drinks (over 65mg per 8oz) using Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) benefits. This new restriction requires federal approval from the USDA and could affect roughly 750,000 residents starting around October 1, 2026, or later, depending on the waiver process.Key Details on Alabama SNAP RestrictionsNew Restrictions (Pending Federal Approval): The law targets the, \"junk food,\" ban on candy, soda, and energy drinks containing stimulants like guarana or taurine.Existing Restrictions: Currently, in line with federal regulations, Alabama SNAP benefits cannot be used for alcohol, tobacco, vitamins, medicine, hot prepared foods, pet food, or non-food household items.Goal: Lawmakers aim to improve public health outcomes by limiting the purchase of high-sugar, low-nutrition items.Exemptions: Sports drinks aimed at hydration, such as Gatorade, are not included in the ban.Effective Date: The state is seeking a waiver, and if approved, the changes are aimed to take effect later in 2026.Current Eligibility RequirementsIncome: For a family of three, gross income cannot exceed $2,694/month (130% of the federal poverty level).Assets: Households must have assets, such as cash or savings, below $2,750.Note: The new restrictions are not yet in effect, as they require approval from the USDA Food and Nutrition Service.",
